# Cinakey — Source of Truth

## 1. Product
Cinakey is the studio pipeline for AI filmmaking: one browser workspace that takes a project from idea to exported cut. Competitors (LTX Studio, Google Flow, Higgsfield) sell access to models; Cinakey wins on pipeline depth: the film is structured, versioned data (script, shots, takes, assets, edit decisions) that the AI copilot can reason over and that can be exported cleanly.
Users: solo creators first (YouTubers, indie filmmakers, ad and social makers); studios and agencies later (phase 3). Core job: "Turn my idea into a finished, consistent video without juggling ten tools or losing track of what worked."
North-star metric: finished minutes exported per active workspace per month.
Built by a solo developer using AI-assisted coding.

## 2. Principles
1. Pipeline over model count: one model per job at launch (Seedance 2.5 video, GPT Image 2 images, DeepSeek copilot) behind model-neutral adapters so more can be added without product changes.
2. Everything is a linked record: every asset knows its project, scene, shot and the job, prompt and references that made it.
3. Nothing is lost: scripts, sheets, shots, blockouts and timelines are versioned with parent pointers; users compare, branch and roll back.
4. The AI works on the project: the copilot reads project data and updates content as new versions with Undo; anything that spends credits shows a cost estimate and asks first. Proposal Accept/Reject remains only for structural steps not yet rewritten to direct apply.
5. Open at the edges: import any media; export MP4 and JSON (later OTIO/XML for Premiere, Resolve, Final Cut).
6. Simple by default: each screen has one job and one main action; minimal text (short labels, helper text one short line at most); advanced options behind a More menu or collapsed section; the copilot owns structure (templates, types, references) so the user never picks categories or fills structured forms; fewer panels and tabs — when in doubt, remove or merge.

## 3. Tech stack and hard rules
- Front end: React + TypeScript (strict), Vite, hosted as a static site (Vercel, Netlify or Cloudflare Pages).
- Back end: Convex (database, queries, mutations, actions, scheduled functions, HTTP actions, file storage, text and vector search). No separate API server.
- Repository: npm workspaces monorepo (`apps/*`, `packages/*`) with Turborepo. `apps/web` is the creator app (`@cinakey/web`); `packages/backend` is the Convex project (`@cinakey/backend`); `packages/shared` holds cross-cutting types (`@cinakey/shared`); `packages/config` holds shared tsconfig / ESLint / Prettier (`@cinakey/config`). Later: `apps/admin` and `packages/ui` (not created yet).
- Auth: Convex Auth (email and password, magic links, Google OAuth). Staff accounts are flagged separately from customers.
- Secrets: provider keys (SEEDANCE_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, etc.) are Convex environment variables, read via process.env only inside actions. Nothing secret in VITE_ variables or the repo.
- Providers: Seedance 2.5 via ByteDance's base API (video, upscale, extend); GPT Image 2 (images, edits); DeepSeek (copilot LLM).
- Storage: Convex file storage behind ONE storage module at `packages/backend/convex/storage.ts` (and matching helpers). Nothing else touches storage directly, so video can later move to Cloudflare R2 (free egress) by changing that module only.
- Convex documents are limited to 1 MiB: large JSON (blockout scenes, timeline snapshots) is stored as files; the database row holds the file ID.
- Rendering happens in the browser: WebCodecs for encode/decode, WebGL/WebGPU for compositing, low-res proxies for playback. No render server in the MVP.
- 3D blockout: three.js used imperatively (scene, stand-ins, props, lights, cameras; dual Shot/Director viewports + TransformControls) + a pure TypeScript animation engine in `@cinakey/shared` (`camAt` / `objAt`, no Theatre.js). Part-scoped `cinakey.blockout/2.0` on `sequences.blockoutFileId`. Blockout MP4 / pre-viz export uses WebCodecs `VideoEncoder` + `mp4-muxer` (video-only); multi-file exports are zipped with JSZip.
- Browser editor (Edit tab): timeline JSON `cinakey.timeline/1.0` on `timelineVersions`; playback uses proxies (Mediabunny demux + WebCodecs when available, HTMLVideoElement fallback) composited with WebGL2, audio from original media via Web Audio with simple music auto-ducking under dialogue; export uses Mediabunny (H.264 + AAC) at up to **1080p / 5 minutes**.
- Real-time comes from Convex live queries (job status, notifications, later presence and shot locks). Yjs for co-editing in phase 3.

## 4. Pipeline
Brief -> Assets -> Script -> Blockout -> Video generation -> Edit -> Review -> Export. Each stage's output is the next stage's structured input; users can enter at any stage (e.g. upload footage and start at Edit). Review notes loop back to single shots (regenerate or swap a take). Project tabs (exactly six): **Copilot · Assets · Script · Blockout · Video · Edit**.

## 5. Modules and features
MVP items are marked [MVP]; others note their phase.
1. Script [MVP]: shared **Prompt Workbench** (left: chat; right: large Seedance script prompt). Prompt **text is the source of truth** (Phase 7C template: REFERENCES with `@image_N`, ART STYLE locked to refs, IMAGE QUALITY, cast/creature/prop blocks copied from asset prompts, LOCATION, timed SHOTS, CONSISTENCY, MOTION AND PHYSICS, LIGHTING, TECHNICAL, MUSIC, AUDIO). Send revises the prompt as a new tip version with Undo. References show as a thumbnail strip; the copilot maps story assets + project style reference to `@image_N`. One part = one video ≤ Seedance `maxDurationSec` (30); longer stories split into Part tabs (backed by `sequences`). Quiet validation one-liner (contiguous shot times, over max duration, unknown `@image_N`). Shots are **parsed from** `Shot N (start–end)` lines and stored as live `shots` for Blockout / Video / Edit — no separate shot-list or storyboard UI on Script. Changing the prompt re-parses shots (stable upsert; unchanged lines keep `blockoutFileId`). Legacy screenplay `scriptVersions` / Script room UI retired (data may remain after migration).
2. Assets [MVP]: creative assets are `entities` (kinds character / creature / location / prop; UI labels environment/product for location/prop). Each asset has a tip `promptSheets` row whose **rendered prompt text is the source of truth** (Phase 7C templates; section validator flags missing headings in one short line). Shared **Prompt Workbench** (left: item chat; right: large editable prompt; Send revises prompt as a new version with Undo; Generate runs GPT Image 2 after cost confirm). No type picker — the copilot detects template type and shows it as a small label. Results under the prompt; user picks one as the reference image (versioned lineage). Project **style reference image** (`projects.styleReferenceAssetId`) at the top of the Assets tab is sent with every GPT Image 2 generation; style entity keeps `artStyleBlock` (ART STYLE paragraph); changing the style reference marks asset sheets style-changed with one-click regenerate. **Saving or revising a script prompt syncs required assets**: REFERENCES / cast / LOCATION create missing entities + draft tips, and mark existing tips `out_of_date` when their script description changed (Assets grid shows “needs update”). **Library** view on the same tab is the uploaded media library (import photos with `likenessConsent`). Legacy Look Dev structured field UI is removed.
3. Blockout [MVP]: built from the script, not planned by hand. Overview shows the **pre-viz player** (`sequences.previzAssetId`) plus primary **Open editor** into the part-scoped 3D editor (`/projects/:projectId/blockout/edit?sequenceId=`). When the script tip changed since the last blockout build, one line **Script changed** + **Update blockout and pre-viz** runs the shot-by-shot **director** (`directPartDocumentFromScript`): reads CAST / LOCATION / timed SHOTS, builds world stand-ins (path, trees, landmarks), densified cast blocking along a travel path, and per-shot camera keys with hold cuts into part `cinakey.blockout/2.0`, then refreshes guides and re-encodes pre-viz. Hand-tuned shot ranges stay via selective keep (`scriptLineKey`); lights-only stubs are fully replaced. Editor chrome: dual Shot cam / Director viewports, left library (characters/props/lights), right Shot/Lens/presets/Export, bottom multi-track timeline; animation via pure shared engine (`camAt`/`objAt`); WebCodecs MP4 export (clay/depth, burn-in) saves to previz. Under More: sheet text, JSON/MP4 export. Legacy `/blockout/shots/:shotId` redirects to edit with `shotId`. True animatic timeline remains phase 2. **Staging is LLM-planned for any script**: DeepSeek writes a `cinakey.staging/1.0` plan (set, cast, per-shot moves and camera; `packages/shared/src/blockout/staging/`), stored on `sequences.stagingPlanFileId`, compiled deterministically by `compileStagingPlan` and checked by `auditStagedDocument` (subject in frame, camera above ground / outside solids). Credits via the chat-job ledger (estimate shown before Update / Regenerate). The rule director (`directPartDocumentFromScript`) is the fallback for mock mode, missing key, insufficient credits or model failure. Theatre.js remains out of scope.
4. Video generation [MVP] (Video tab): one main action **Generate video** per Part (sequence) using the approved script prompt, asset reference images, project style reference, and blockout guide renders (within Seedance `maxReferenceImages`). Optional More checkbox **Use pre-viz as reference video** when `previzAssetId` exists (passes `referenceVideoUrl` into Seedance). Cost estimate + confirm before run. Gallery of every sequence-master video for the Part (newest first): play, compare two side by side, mark **Chosen** (`sequences.chosenMasterAssetId`), generate again. Under each video's More: upscale, extend (Seedance 2.5), download, delete. Choosing a master selects its trimmed per-shot takes (`trimStartSec`/`trimEndSec` from script timings) so Edit can assemble. No per-shot take management UI. Failed jobs refund credits. Batch scene generation and lip sync are phase 2.
5. Browser editor [MVP, in-house]: Edit tab (`/projects/:projectId/edit`) over `cinakey.timeline/1.0` JSON on `timelineVersions` (file-backed via storage). Multi-track timeline (video, dialogue, music, SFX, titles); **Assemble from script** places the Chosen video's trimmed shots in order (selected takes, planned durations, captions from script dialogue). Default chrome: timeline + transport + Assemble + Export; Media bin and Clip inspector behind More/panels. Cut, ripple/roll trim, split, move, delete, reorder; zoom, snap, playhead; keyboard J/K/L, I/O, S, Delete, Space, undo/redo; music upload, per-clip volume/fades, auto-duck music under dialogue; title/caption styles (`lower-third`, `centered`, `caption`); debounced autosave as new timeline versions + history restore; in-browser MP4 export (originals, WebCodecs + Mediabunny, progress/cancel/beforeunload) capped at **1080p long edge and 5 minutes**. Known limits: smooth scrub target ~2 min / ~30 clips; proxies are muted/low-res (preview ≠ export colour); Chromium recommended for encode. Aspect-ratio variants, colour/LUTs and OTIO/XML export are phase 2; migrating blockout export off deprecated `mp4-muxer` to Mediabunny is deferred.
6. Media library [MVP]: reachable as the **Library** view on the Assets tab (and workspace-wide `/assets`); upload footage, photos, audio, music and logos (MIME + size validation via the storage module); typed media assets linked to project/scene/shot/job with display `name` and searchable tags; grid and list views; filters by type, tag, character (via shot), shot; text search; detail drawer with preview, metadata, tags, and lineage; star, rename, tag, delete with confirm. Selected takes kept; rejected (unselected) takes purged after **30 days** unless starred (daily cron via `takes.purgeRejectedTakes`).
7. AI copilot [MVP]: DeepSeek via its API behind an adapter; **one conversation per project** — full-page on the Copilot tab, slim side panel on every other tab; knows which tab and item is open (`view` + `selectionIds`); no director/writer/character roles, mode switchers, or pipeline panel. Tools apply prompt edits **directly** as new versions with Undo; credit-spending tools (`generate_image`, `generate_video`) estimate cost and confirm first. Core tools: `write_or_revise_asset_prompt`, `write_or_revise_script_prompt`, `update_blockout`, `generate_image`, `generate_video`, `update_rules`, project read helpers. Project rules (`projects.rules`) are a short editable list under More. Streaming via live-updated `copilotMessages`; each turn reserved/settled on the credits ledger via slim `generationJobs` kind `chat`. Deferred: multi-thread chat, mobile side-panel Sheet.
8. Platform essentials [MVP]: cost estimates, confirmation when a run exceeds `COST_CONFIRM_THRESHOLD_CREDITS` (50), optional per-project spend caps (`projects.spendCapCredits`) that block new jobs once reached; in-app **and email** job/export notifications (Resend; toggle in Settings); automatic retries and credit refunds; guided **starter template** opens straight into the Script tab with a sample copilot conversation (no checklist); one-time **100-credit** starter grant per workspace; protected staff pages at `/staff` (user lookup, credit grant/refund with required reason, moderation queue, provider health, monthly egress, audit log) with `requireStaff` on every staff function and append-only `auditLog`; Settings (profile, email prefs, credit history, delete account with data deletion); egress events for storage/playback/export; rate limits on expensive actions; analyticsEvents for activation and finished export minutes. New project: **name + aspect ratio only** (fps defaults to 24; brief/structure via copilot). Share links and project members stay phase 2. Full admin platform with RBAC stays phase 2 (`apps/admin`).
9. Admin platform (phase 2): separate React app in `apps/admin` on the same Convex deployment; areas for users, projects, jobs/providers, billing/credits, moderation, models/pricing, analytics, admin users, audit log; RBAC roles (Super admin, Operations admin, Support, Moderator, Finance, Engineer, Analyst) built from fine-grained permissions; every admin function checks permissions server-side; append-only audit log.

## 6. Data model (Convex tables)
MVP ownership: each user has a personal `workspace`; every `project` belongs to a workspace; access = workspace owner (or `users.isStaff`). `projectMembers` / share links / admin RBAC come later.

Versioning: `scriptVersions` (legacy screenplay blobs; UI retired) and `timelineVersions` insert new rows with optional `parentId` (never overwrite content in place). Script prompts live on tip `promptSheets` (`type: "script"`) with **rendered text as source of truth**; structured JSON is derived on save for bridges. Timeline body is `cinakey.timeline/1.0` JSON in file storage (`timelineFileId`). Creative assets are `entities`; optional legacy Look Dev `cinakey.sheet/1.0` blobs may remain on `entities.sheetFileId`. Blockout JSON is a new storage blob per save (`cinakey.blockout/1.0` single-shot document with `version` and `parentFileId`); `shots.blockoutFileId` points at the current file. **Prompt sheets** (`promptSheets`) version via tip rows (`isTip`, `parentId`, `version`); for asset and script types **rendered prompt text is the source of truth**. Blockout sheets remain structured-first (rewritten on Update). Live working records (`scenes`, `shots`, `sequences` as Parts) may be patched. Large JSON always lives in file storage; documents hold only storage IDs (`sizeBytes` required on assets). Credits are per workspace (`creditLedger` append-only). Soft-archive projects via `archivedAt`. Account delete hard-deletes the caller’s personal workspace tree. Copilot conversation is one linear thread per project (`copilotMessages`); credit-spending and remaining structural tools may still create `proposals`.

